ERR1 = "This parameter shares its subtype with another writable parameter, a bounded error if the same actual is passed for both"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_7.1.8",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_7.1.8": "Published Standards/Ada 95/7.1.8 Dependence on Parameter Passing Mechanism",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_7.1.8": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Portability",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_7.1.8": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Do not write code whose correct execution depends on the
particular parameter passing mechanism used by an implementation (Ada
Reference Manual, &sect;6.2; Cohen 1986).</p>
<p>&#8226; If a subprogram has more than one formal parameter of a given
subtype, at least one of which is [in] out, make sure that the
subprogram can properly handle the case when both formal parameters
denote the same actual object.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>The output of this program depends on the particular parameter
passing mechanism that was used:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">------------------------------------------------------------------------
with Ada.Integer_Text_IO;
procedure Outer is
   type Coordinates is
      record
         X : Integer := 0;
         Y : Integer := 0;
      end record;
   Outer_Point : Coordinates;
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   procedure Inner (Inner_Point : in out Coordinates) is
   begin
      Inner_Point.X := 5;
      -- The following line causes the output of the program to
      -- depend on the parameter passing mechanism.
      Ada.Integer_Text_IO.Put(Outer_Point.X);
   end Inner;
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
begin  -- Outer
   Ada.Integer_Text_IO.Put(Outer_Point.X);
   Inner(Outer_Point);
   Ada.Integer_Text_IO.Put(Outer_Point.X);
end Outer;
------------------------------------------------------------------------
</code></pre>
<p>If the parameter passing mechanism is by copy, the results on the
standard output file are:</p>
<p>0 0 5</p>
<p>If the parameter passing mechanism is by reference, the results are:</p>
<p>0 5 5</p>
<p>The following code fragment shows where there is a potential for
bounded error when a procedure is called with actual parameters denoting
the same object:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">procedure Test_Bounded_Error (Parm_1 : in out    Integer;
                              Parm_2 : in out Integer) is
   procedure Inner (Parm : in out Integer) is
   begin
      Parm := Parm * 10;
   end Inner;
begin
   Parm_2 := 5;
   Inner (Parm_1);
end Test_Bounded_Error;
</code></pre>
<p>In executing the procedure Test_Bounded_Error, both Parm_1 and Parm_2
denote the object Actual_Parm. After executing the first statement, the
object Actual_Parm has the value 5. When the procedure Inner is called,
its formal parameter Parm denotes Actual_Parm. It cannot be determined
whether it denotes the old value of Parm_1, in this case 1, or the new
value, in this case 5.</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">Actual_Parm : Integer := 1;
...
Test_Bounded_Error (Actual_Parm, Actual_Parm);  -- potential bounded error
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>Certain composite types (untagged records and arrays) can be passed
either by copy or by reference. If there are two or more formal
parameters of the same type, one or more of which is writable, then you
should document whether you assume that these formal parameters do not
denote the same actual object. Similarly, if a subprogram that has a
formal parameter of a given subtype also makes an up-level reference to
an object of this same type, you should document whether you assume that
the formal parameter denotes a different object from the object named in
the up-level reference. In these situations where an object can be
accessed through distinct formal parameter paths, the exception
Program_Error may be raised, the new value may be read, or the old value
of the object may be used (Ada Reference Manual, &sect;6.2).</p>
<p>See also Guideline 8.2.7.</p>

<p><b>Exceptions</b></p>
<p>Frequently, when interfacing Ada to foreign code, dependence on
parameter-passing mechanisms used by a particular implementation is
unavoidable. In this case, isolate the calls to the foreign code in an
interface package that exports operations that do not depend on the
parameter-passing mechanism.</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>Only the second bullet is automated: a procedure or entry with two or
more formal parameters of the same subtype, at least one of which is
<code>out</code> or <code>in out</code>, is a candidate, using the same mode-detection
technique <code>ADA95_5.2.4</code> uses (Understand's Ada parameter entity does
not expose its mode through <code>kind()</code>, so the lexer is scanned past
the parameter's <code>:</code> for an explicit <code>in</code>/<code>out</code> keyword). This
matches the guideline's own wording literally: the hazard it names
applies to two formal parameters sharing a subtype regardless of
whether that subtype happens to be a record, array, or scalar type
&#8212; the guideline's own example uses plain <code>Integer</code> parameters. An
<code>access</code> parameter is not counted as writable for this purpose,
since the aliasing it names is a mechanism ambiguity in how the
designated object is reached, not something an access value (always
passed by copy of the pointer itself) is subject to.</p>
<p>Within a candidate group, only the specific parameters that show an
<code>Assignby Ref</code> reference (such as <code>CallParamFormal Assignby
Ref</code>) are flagged, not every parameter in the group regardless of use.
Understand produces this kind when a parameter is accessed through a
reference-capable path &#8212; passed on as an actual to another call's
writable parameter (the guideline's own <code>Inner (Parm_1)</code> example), or
referenced up-level from a nested subprogram, as happens when a record
or array <code>out</code> parameter is sliced and assigned from within a local
procedure defined inside the body. A bare <code>Ref</code> kind (without
<code>Assignby</code>) also exists but is deliberately not used, since it can
appear for unrelated reasons, such as on an imported/foreign
subprogram's own parameter declaration. An ordinary direct read or
write of the parameter itself, a read-only pass-through to another
call's <code>in</code> parameter, or access to one of its own components,
does not produce <code>Assignby Ref</code>. This narrows the check to
parameters that actually
participate in the hazard shape rather than every same-subtype
parameter regardless of whether it is used at all, at the cost of
still not proving that any specific call site passes the same actual
object to two of them, nor that the subprogram's body would actually
misbehave if it did &#8212; proving either would require tracing every call
site and the body's own data flow, matching how <code>ADA95_6.2.4</code> flags a
shared variable as a structural proxy without proving an actual race.</p>
<p>The first bullet is not automated: the hazard in its own example (an
up-level reference inside a nested subprogram that may or may not denote
the same object as an enclosing <code>in out</code> parameter) can only be
proven by tracing whether a specific call site's actual object is the
very one being referenced up-level, which is an interprocedural
alias/data-flow problem, not a structural pattern.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


def _mode(lexer, line, col):
    lex = lexer.lexeme(line, col)

    if not lex:
        return "in"

    while lex and not (lex.token() == "Punctuation" and lex.text() == ":"):
        lex = lex.next(True, True)

    if not lex:
        return "in"

    lex = lex.next(True, True)

    if not lex:
        return "in"

    if lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text() == "access":
        return "access"

    if lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text() == "out":
        return "out"

    if lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text() == "in":
        nxt = lex.next(True, True)

        if nxt and nxt.token() == "Keyword" and nxt.text() == "out":
            return "in out"

        return "in"

    return "in"


def _is_writable(mode):
    return mode in ("out", "in out")


def _passed_by_ref(param_ent):
    # "Assignby Ref" (e.g. "CallParamFormal Assignby Ref") only appears
    # when a parameter is accessed through a reference-capable path, such
    # as being passed on to another call's writable parameter or referenced
    # up-level from a nested subprogram - the same aliasing shape as the
    # guideline's own Inner(Parm_1) example. A bare "Ref" (without
    # "Assignby") also exists but can appear for unrelated reasons, such as
    # on an imported/foreign subprogram's own parameter declaration, so it
    # is deliberately not used here. Ordinary direct reads/writes of the
    # parameter, a read-only pass-through, or access to one of its own
    # components, never produce "Assignby Ref", so this narrows the check
    # to parameters that actually participate in the hazard instead of
    # every parameter in a same-subtype group regardless of use.
    return bool(param_ent.refs("Assignby Ref"))


def check(check, file):
    filename = file.longname()
    lexer = None
    checked = set()

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare", "Procedure, Entry", False):
        subprog_ent = decl_ref.ent()
        ent_id = subprog_ent.id()

        if ent_id in checked:
            continue

        checked.add(ent_id)

        has_spec_elsewhere = any(
            r.file().longname().endswith(".ads") and r.file().longname() != filename
            for r in subprog_ent.refs("Declare", "Parameter", False)
        )

        if has_spec_elsewhere:
            continue

        seen_param_ids = set()
        params = []

        for r in sorted(
            subprog_ent.refs("Declare", "Parameter", False),
            key=lambda r: (r.line(), r.column()),
        ):
            if r.file().longname() != filename or r.ent().id() in seen_param_ids:
                continue

            seen_param_ids.add(r.ent().id())
            params.append(r)

        if not params:
            continue

        if lexer is None:
            lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)

        groups = {}

        for param_ref in params:
            type_ref = param_ref.ent().ref("Typed", "Type")

            if not type_ref:
                continue

            mode = _mode(lexer, param_ref.line(), param_ref.column())
            groups.setdefault(type_ref.ent().id(), []).append((param_ref, mode))

        for entries in groups.values():
            if len(entries) < 2:
                continue

            if not any(_is_writable(mode) for _, mode in entries):
                continue

            for param_ref, _ in entries:
                if _passed_by_ref(param_ref.ent()):
                    check.violation(param_ref.ent(), file, param_ref.line(),
                                    param_ref.column(), ERR1)
